\check Mid-sentence Capital
\submenu Phil_new
\script mid_sentence_capital.py
\description Flags a capital letter that appears mid-sentence: after a comma, semicolon, or another word rather than after sentence-final punctuation. Ignores capitalized words found in the project TermRenderings.xml. Options let you set sentence-boundary characters, words/prefixes/suffixes to ignore, quote-opening handling, and character/paragraph styles to skip.
\books
\noText
\toList
\utf8

\optionName FinalPunctuation
\optionLocalizedName Sentence boundary characters
\optionDescription Characters that mark a sentence boundary. Capitalized words immediately following any of these are not flagged. Default covers . ! ?; inverted sentence-opening punctuation is handled automatically.
\optionDefault .!?

\optionName WordsToIgnore
\optionLocalizedName Words to ignore
\optionDescription Comma-separated list of capitalized words that should not be flagged (e.g. Senor, Alfa, Espiritu). Use this for proper nouns not covered by TermRenderings.xml.
\optionDefault

\optionName IgnoreCapAfterQuote
\optionLocalizedName Ignore capital after opening quote
\optionDescription Set to Yes to suppress errors where a capitalized word follows an opening quotation mark (e.g. direct speech after a comma). The characters treated as openers are configured by the Opening quote characters option below.
\optionDefault No

\optionName QuoteOpeningChars
\optionLocalizedName Opening quote characters
\optionDescription Characters treated as opening quotation marks when "Ignore capital after opening quote" is set to Yes. Each character in this string is an opener. Default covers left guillemet («), left double curly quote (“), em dash (—), and left single curly quote (‘). Clear this field to fall back to Unicode Pi-category detection.
\optionDefault «“—‘

\optionName IgnoreStyles
\optionLocalizedName Character styles to ignore
\optionDescription Comma-separated USFM character style names (no backslash) whose enclosed text is not checked for mid-sentence capitals and is excluded from end-of-verse detection. Use for paired styles like qs (Selah) or pn (proper names) that contain capitalized words that should not be flagged.
\optionDefault

\optionName IgnoreParaStyles
\optionLocalizedName Paragraph styles to ignore
\optionDescription Comma-separated USFM paragraph marker names (no backslash) whose text is treated as a sentence boundary and excluded from end-of-verse detection. Default covers: sp (speaker), r (parallel reference), ms/mr (major section), sr (section range). Section headings (s, s1, s2...) are suppressed automatically.
\optionDefault sp, r, ms, mr, sr

\optionName WordPrefixes
\optionLocalizedName Word prefixes to ignore
\optionDescription Space-separated list of word prefixes (e.g. ra Ra). A capitalized word beginning with any of these (case-insensitive) will not be flagged.
\optionDefault

\optionName WordSuffixes
\optionLocalizedName Word suffixes to ignore
\optionDescription Space-separated list of word suffixes (e.g. es am). A capitalized word ending with any of these (case-insensitive) will not be flagged.
\optionDefault

\optionName MidWordPunctuation
\optionLocalizedName Mid-word punctuation
\optionDescription Characters that may appear inside a word (e.g. - for hyphen, ' for apostrophe). Enter without spaces. Words containing these characters are treated as a single unit when checking for capitals.
\optionDefault -

